window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2022-05-17"]={"AD-009-22":"Resolution No. AD-009-22\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nDISTmBUTION OF MORTGAGE TAX\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has received mortgage tax monies for the period October, 2021 through March, 2022 in the amount of $5,789,733.28, and\nWHEREAS, the Recording Officer has previously distributed $1,417,523.57 to the Niagara Frontier Transportation Authority, $1,346,984.55 to the State Mortgage Tax Agency, and retained $129,453.01 for\napproved county administrative expenses, and\nWHEREAS, that Recording Officer has remitted the remaining monies collected to the County Treasurer for distribution to various Niagara County towns, villages and cities, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $2,895,772.15 reflects mortgage tax monies for the period October 1, 2021 through March 31, 2022 to be distributed, and the same be and hereby is, apportioned as follows among the various towns, villages and cities of the County of Niagara:\nTOWNS  Cambria  86,212.45\nHartland  35,311.88\nLewiston  249,611.02\nLockport  850,329.49\nNewfane  77,719.75\nNiagara  52,627.67\nPendleton  161,229.86\nPorter  66,819.13\nRoyalton  70J52.53\nSomerset  24,690.64\nWheatfield  283J15.56\nWilson  73,844.23\nVILLAGES  Middleport (Hartland)  $ 551.29\nMiddleport (Royalton)  5,347.96\nLewiston  30,608.99\nYoungstown  12,722.05\nBarker  1,708.78\nWilson  8,071.30\nCITIES  Lockport  $ 195,993.35\nNiagara Falls  306,464.34\nNorth Tonawanda  302.039.88\nTOTAL:  $2,895,772.15\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the County Treasurer be, and hereby is, directed to pay the Supervisors of the various towns, village treasurers, and city treasurers the amounts recorded above and that this document shall be sufficient authorization to the County Treasurer to make the payments in accordance with the above direction.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","AD-010-22":"Resolution No. AD-010-22\nFrom: Admimstration Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nUPGRADE WITH ADJUSTED WAGE FOR SENIOR INSURANCE PROGRAM ASSISTANT\nPOSITION OFFICE OF RISK MANAGEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Office of Risk Management has had two long-term employees vacate and / or retire from the Principal Insurance Program Assistant and eliminated one Senior Insurance Program Assistant position, leaving one Senior Insurance Program Assistant exclusively dedicated to the leadership role in the Risk, healthcare administrative function, and\nWHEREAS, a critical function of this position is to administer Open Enrollment annually in the fall for Healthcare benefits, for both active employees and retirees, and ongoing daily administration of these benefits, healthcare eligibility and contribution schedules, and\nWHEREAS, recent settlement of union contracts and changes in healthcare law resulted in Plan changes and administrative complexities, and\nWHEREAS, in order to provide training and oversight of this complex function to subordinate staff, the Office of Risk Management seeks to upgrade this position and pay accordingly, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that upon assessing this function and the longstanding job history of the Senior Insurance Program Assistant incumbent of the Office and veteran Niagara County Health Plan administrative expert with the Human Resources Director and County Manager, it has been determined that this position of Senior Insurance Program Assistant be granted an upgrade to Group 10, Step 3 ($27.94/hr) effective May 01, 2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this position will be paid at the upgrade and will be funded from the personnel line, position number 13278, and the funds will continue to be reimbursed through the line MI.13.1710.000 74905.00, Self-Insured Health Plan, Administrative Support Expense, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.13.1430.106 412889.09 Salary Reimbursement $1,933\nINCREASE EXPENSE:\nA.13.1430.106 71010.00 Positions $1,568\nA.13.1430.106 78100.00 Retirement 204\nA.13.1430.106 78200.00 FICA 120\nA.13.1430.106 78300.00 Worker's Comp 41\nMoved by Godfrey, seconded by Nemi to table the resolution.\nCarried. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CS-019-22":"Resolution No. CS-019-22\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY YOUTH BUREAU - NEW YORK STATE AID APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has been designated as eligible to receive state aid for 2022 youth programs through its Youth Bureau from the New York State Office of Children and Family Services in the amount of $255,750, and\nWHEREAS, the Comprehensive Planning Law mandates the Niagara County Youth Bureau must apply for such funds, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review the documents for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of the County Youth Bureau be, and hereby is, authorized to make application for the following program funds: Youth Development Program $255,750 and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute any and all documents relating to the application, subject to the review by the County Manager and the approval of the County Attorney.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CS-020-22":"Resolution No. CS-020-22\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nCOUNTY CLERK OFFICE CONTRACT AMENDMENT\nWHEREAS, the appropriate, accurate and timely management of local government records is essential for efficient and effective government, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Clerk's Office has experienced a significant backlog in the indexing and verification of legal land documents and records, and\nWHEREAS, said backlog of indexing and verification of legal land documents and records was caused by extraordinary circumstances including, but not limited to, multiple retirements, resignations, COVID -19 related staff shortages as well as numerous other unforeseen staffing challenges, and\nWHEREAS, despite increased overtime efforts by the Niagara County Clerk's Office Staff, the said backlog persists and is perpetuating, and\nWHEREAS, it has been mutually agreed by Niagara County and the Union that an Amendment to the Contract between Niagara County and Info Quick Solutions, Inc. dated February 18, 2014 is both necessary, practical, and desirable to address the backlog, and\nWHEREAS, Info Quick Solutions, Inc. possesses the necessary skill and expertise to eliminate the aforementioned backlog of indexing and verification of legal land documents and records, and\nWHEREAS, except for this Amendment, all other terms and Amendments to the original Contract dated February 18, 2014 shall remain unchanged and in force, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County enter an Amendment to the Contract with Info Quick-Solutions, Inc. dated April 27, 2022 to provide indexing and verification services of the aforementioned backlogged documents for the period commencing January 14, 2022 and terminating April 20, 2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that any time extensions to the January 14, 2022 - April 20, 2022 time period will require a mutually agreed upon written amendment signed by authorized representatives of the County and the Union, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the Amendment, subject to the review by the County Manager and the approval of the County Attorney, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer's Office is hereby authorized to disburse an amount not to exceed $13,300.00 from the County Clerk's Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds Account A.10.1410 40599.01 into the County Clerk's operating budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nLNCREASE REVENUE:\nAJO.1410.000 40599.01 Appropriated Fund Balance-Committed Funds $13,300.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.10.1410.000 74500.01 Contractual Expenses $13,300.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CS-021-22":"Resolution No. CS-021-22\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nABOLISH (1) FT HEAD COOK\nCREATE AND FILL (I) FT COOK and (1) FT FOOD SERVICE DIRECTOR\nWHEREAS, the department has reviewed its operational needs in order to improve efficiencies and provide nutritionally balanced meals to the older adults that rely on the valuable services offered through the OFA, and\nWHEREAS, after studying the operation in the kitchen and evaluating all options, it has been determined that it would be in the best interest of the department to abolish one (1) FT Head Cook and to create and fill one (1) FT Food Service Director, and (1) FT Cook position, and\nWHEREAS, funding for this newly created position is readily available In the current budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Position No. 3898, Head Cook, AFSCME, be abolished, effective May 29, 2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the position of FT Food Service Director, 40 hours per week, position # xxx. Flat Salary, Grade 6 Step 4, be created and filled, effective May 29,2022,and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the position of FT Cook, 40 hours per week, position # xxx, AFSCME be created and filled, effective May 29, 2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated.\nDECREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.24.6772.000 71030.01 Part Time Expense $ 7,925\nPosition #10254\nA.24.7610.702 71030.01 Part Time Expense $62,838\nPosition #3709 $10,959\nPosition #1348 $15,985\nPosition #1349 $ 5,716\nPosition $1358 $ 1,188\nPosition #1368 $ 2,275\nPosition #1362 $ 1,907\nPosition $1376 $ 206\nPosition #3 898 $24,602\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.24.7610.702 71010.00 Positions Expense $ 56,653\nPosition # xxx (FT Cook) $ 24,602\nPosition # xxx (FS Director) $ 32,051\nA.24.7610.702 78100.00 Retirement Expense $ 2,628\nA 24.7610.702 78400.01 Insurance, Health Active Expense $ 7,307\nA 24.7610.702 78400.05 Insurance, Health HRA Employer Contribution $ 496\nA 24.7610.702 78800.00 Flex 125 Employer Contribution Expense $ 403\nA.24.7610.702 78200.00 FICA Expense $ 2,452\nA.24.7610.702 78300.00 Worker's Compensation $ 824\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CSS-033-22":"Resolution No. CSS-033-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nAID TO DEFENSE PROGRAM RENEWAL 2022-2023\nPUBLIC DEFENDER\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Defender's Office has been awarded renewed funding from the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services under the Aid to Defense Program for the purpose of continuing aid in the defense of repeat violent and serious felony offenders in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, funding has been awarded in the amount of $15,980 for the period April 1, 2022 through March 31, 2023, with no matching funds requirement, and\nWHEREAS, the funds have been appropriated in the Niagara County Public Defender's 2022 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept the renewal of this grant, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant award contract, the County Attorney will review the grant award contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the grant award documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CSS-034-22":"Resolution No. CSS-034-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17,2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY FIRE COORDINATOR'S OFFICE\n2022 BUDGET MODIFICATION\nPUBLIC SAFETY RADIO SYSTEM EXPANSION AND UPGRADES\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office operates the Dispatch Center for police and fire dispatch located at the Niagara County Public Safety Training Facility, and\nWHEREAS, the Dispatch Center utilizes UHF P25 truriked public safety communications system to dispatch mcoming 911 calls, and\nWHEREAS, the proposal from Mission Critical Partners, LLC is to support the continued expansion of the UHF P25 trunked public safety communications system, geo-redundant prime implementation, mteroperability sub\" system implementation, and fire paging sub-system upgrade; the scope of work will include the three new sites Wilson, Shawnee, afid Barker, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-015-18 on February 20, 2018 to use wireless E911 surcharge revenue to fund projects related to wireless 911 service to the coimnunity, and\nWHEREAS, the excess funds from surcharges were reserved in committed fund balance and are available to carry over, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorize the Chairman of the Legislature to enter into a contract with Mission Critical Partners, LLC to implement services to the expansion of the Niagara County UHF P25 trunked radio project, not to exceed $214,230.00 said contract to be subject to the review of the County Attorney for compliance with legal form and County policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.19.3020.000 40599.01 Appropriated Fund Balance-Comnutted Funds $214,230\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.19.3020.000 74500.01 Contractual Expenses $214,230\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CSS-035-22":"Resolution No. CSS-035-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17,2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nEXTENSION OF FORENSIC FIREARMS CONSULTANT AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has entered into a Consulting Agreement with a consultant for forensic firearms related testing and examinations for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office, and\nWHEREAS, the current agreement was entered into with Mark Shaw, and\nWHEREAS, the current agreement is dated May 1, 2017 and has a term period of two (2) years from that date with the option of extending the agreement by an amendment, and\nWHEREAS, the County wishes to extend the agreement with Mark Shaw for a fourth one-year extension period, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature has determined that the services to be provided by the consultant requires a high degree of specialized skill constituting \"professional services\" and hereby waives any requirement that additional proposals must be obtained or solicited, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that County Attorney will draft an amendment for a one-year extension, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the amendment to extend the Consulting Agreement.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CSS-036-22":"Resolution No. CSS-036-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17,2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEmFF'S OFFICE\nEXTEND FY19 OPERATION STONEGARDEN GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed a resolution CSS-024-20 on May 12, 2020 to accept $257,800 under the FY2019 Operation Stonegarden Program, funding provided by the U.S Department of Homeland Security Federal Management Agency, and\nWHEREAS, Operation Stonegarden has been awarded to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office for many years, and\nWHEREAS, the performance period for this grant is September 1, 2019 through August 31, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, the grant is used for expenses to assist the County in conducting border centric, intelligence driven operations with the goal of reduction or elimination of threat, risk and vulnerability along our Nation's borders, and\nWHEREAS, the grant performance period has been extended to August 31, 2023, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this grant\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CSS-037-22":"Resolution No. CSS-037-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17,2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nCONTRACT FOR AXON CAMERAS AND STORAGE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed a resolution CSS-038-19 on June 18, 2019 to standardize Axon as the sole source for in-car cameras, body cameras, related hardware, software and digital storage services, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been in negotiations with Axon to achieve the most fiscally responsible contract possible for renewal of in-car cameras, body cameras, .interview cameras and combined digital storage, and\nWHEREAS, the Axon and the Niagara County Sheriff's Office has come to a mutual agreement for a 5 year contract under the Sole Source agreement, and\nWHEREAS, no additional funds are needed in the 2022 budget for the agreement, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this contract.\nMoved by Bradt, Seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CSS-038-22":"Resolution No. CSS-038-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17,2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEMFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - EXPENDITURE OF CELLULAR E911 REVENUE\nFOR COMMU1SICATIONS TRUNKING EXPANSION SOLUTION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed a resolution CSS-040-21 on April 20, 2021 to approve the project to enhance the County-wide Public Safety Radio Network (the \"System\") which was designed and implemented by Motorola Solutions (\"Motorola\"), by constructing radio towers, installation of facilities and purchase of equipment for three new radio tower locations in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, Motorola and the County's independent consultant, Mission Critical Partners, LLC (\"Mission Critical\") have recommended an enhancement to the County's System by way of an optimized 3-site Trunking Expansion Solution which will expand the Niagara County trunked simulcast system to a total of 11 tower sites which will result in improved communications coverage and safety for all users of the system, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed a Local Law on November 5, 2017 which established an E911 surcharge for cellular phones to establish a dedicated revenue to be used for future payments for system costs, eligible wireless 911 service costs, or other costs associated with the administration, design, enhanced wireless 911 service costs serving Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, such expenditures require the authorization of the Niagara County Legislature, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office has secured funding for the project through various funding sources to include Statewide Interoperable Communications Grants FY19 and FY20, Narrowbanding Capital Project and 911 Wireless Funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the Project and directs the Sheriffs Office and the County Manager to take such steps as are necessary to carry it out, including purchasing equipment, materials and services as necessary from pertinent New York State Office of General Services\napproved contracts from Motorola, in an amount not to exceed $1,724,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following line item transfers be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.19.3020.000 40599.01 Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds $ 806,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.19.3020.000 72100.15 Communications Equipment $ 806,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CW-010-22":"Resolution No. CW-010-22\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nTRANSFER OF 6805 DAY ROAD PROPERTY HOME RULE MESSAGE\nSENATE BILL NO. S.6313-B AND ASSEMBLY BILL NO. A.7837-B\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara received certain lands from transfers In 1962 on the condition that they be used for park and recreational purposes, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has not pursued the development of these lands and does not have any immediate plans to do so and the Town of Lockport expressed an interest in November of 2020 in acquiring this land and developing on the land, park and recreational activities, and\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-013-21, the County authorized the entering into an agreement to transfer premises known as 6805 Day Road, identified by Tax SBL #110.00-1-30.1, and requesting the New York State Senate and the New York State Assembly to initiate legislation; to execute all further necessary paperwork to accomplish the transfer pursuant to that agreement, and\nWHEREAS, the property is further described m the following proposed legal description:\nALL THAT TRACT OR PARCEL OF LAND situated in the Town ofLockport, County of Niagara, and State of New York, being part of Lot 8, Section 10, Township 14, Range 6 of the Holland Land Company's Sm-vey, more particularly described as follows: Commencing at a point of intersection of the centeriine of East High Street (being 66.0 feet wide), also being the south line of Lot 8 and the extension of the west line of Day Road (being 66.0 feet wide), said point also being the southeasterly comer of lands conveyed to the Town of Lockport as filed by deed in the Niagara County Clerk's Office in Liber 3378 of Deeds at Page 281; Thence, N 06\u00b0 -49' \"50\" W, along the west line of Day road, also being the east line said lands conveyed to the Town ofLockport, a distance of 1780.00 feet to the Point of Beginning; Thence, N 90\u00b0 -00' -00\" W, parallel with the centerline of East High Street, also being the north line said lands conveyed to the Town ofLockport, a distance of 1190.73 feet to a point on the west line Lot 8; Thence, N 05\u00b0 -49' -31\" W, along said west line Lot 8, a distance of 2213.82 feet to a point on the north line Lot 8; Thence, N 89\u00b0 -55' -46\" E, along said north line Lot 8, a distance of 1151.45 feet to a point on the west line Day Road, also bemg the west line lands conveyed to the Town ofLockport as filed by deed in the Niagara County Clerk's Office in Liber 1295 of Deeds at Page 105; Thence, S 06\u00b0 -49' -50\" E, along said west line of lands conveyed to the Town of Lockport, a distance of 2219.56 feet to the Point of Beginning. Containing 58.23 acres of land, more or less.\nWHEREAS, as part of the agreement of transfer, the Town of Lockport shall continue to make all park areas and improvements equally available to both Town and County residents and shall not enact any fees or charges which are higher for any County residents who are not Town residents, and\nWHEREAS, the Town ofLockport shall continue to comply with Article 6-C of the Conservation Law of the State of New York as it existed at the time the County acquired title to said property, and\nWHEREAS, the transfer of the Day Road property is dependent upon the approval of the New York State Legislature, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that pursuant to Article D< of the Constitution, the Niagara County Legislature requests that the New York State Legislature enact legislation, Senate Bill S.6313-B and Assembly Bill A.7837-B, enabling the transfer of 6805 Day Road property, in the Town ofLockport (SBL #110.00-1-30.1) to the Town ofLockport, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that copies of this resolution be forwarded to the New York State Senate, the New York State Assembly, Senator Robert G. Ortt, Assemblyman Michael J. Norris and Assemblyman Angelo J. Mormello.\nMoved by Collins, seconded by Grozio to amend resolution.\nAmendment: Deletion of B m Bill numbers and replace with C, in the title and the first RESOLVED in the First\nRESOLVED.\nMoved by Collins, seconded by Grozio.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~ Myers.","CW-011-22":"Resolution No. CW-011-22\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nHOME RULE MESSAGE\nREQUESTING AN ACT TO AUTHORIZE GEORGE A. AMENDOLA\n(RETIREE OF THE NIAGARA FALLS SCHOOL DISTRICT) TIER H STATUS\nIN THE NEW YORK STATE AND LOCAL EMPLOYEES^ RETIRMENT SYSTEM\nWHEREAS, legislation has been introduced in the New York State Senate relating to the New York state and local employees' retirement system tier status of George A. Amendola, an employee of the Niagara Falls school district, and\nWHEREAS, George A. Amendola was first eligible to join the public retirement system as of September 20, 1974, and for reasons not ascribable to his own negligence he was not afforded the opportunity to join the New York state and local employees' retirement system with tier 2 status, and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to Legislative Law Section 50 the bill will grant tier 2 status to George A. Amendolas, a retired Tier 4 member of the New York State and Local Employee's Retirement System, doing so by changing his date of membership to September 20, 1974, his first date of employment with the Niagara Falls School District, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature supports Senate Bill No.. 5101 permitting George A. Amendola's tier status to be changed, with no contributions made shall be returned or refunded to Mr. Amendolas pursuant to the act, and the past service costs associated with the implementation of act shall be borne by the Niagara Falls school district, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Niagara County Legislature is hereby authorized and directed to execute the proper Home Rule documents and immediately forward them to the New York State Legislature.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CW-012-22":"Resolution No. CW-012-22\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: May 17,2022\nAWARD OF CONTRACT - CULVERT REPLACEMENTS 2022\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Culvert Replacements 2022 Project, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on May 12, 2022 as tabulated below: 1. Edbauer Construction $378,657.00 2790 Clinton Street West Seneca, NY 14224 2. Occhino Corp. $382,960.98 2650 Seneca Street West Seneca, NY 14224 3. 4th Generation Construction $392,740.00 5650 Simmons Avenue Niagara Falls, NY 14304 4. J. Bognar Construction $396,921.74 P.O. Box 4 Sardinia,NY 14134 and\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE: H743.15.5197.000 43501.00 Consol Highway Aid Revenue $400,000 INCREASE APPROPRIATIONS: H743.15.5197.000 72600.02 Bridges $400,000 and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Culvert Replacements 2022 Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clmton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, in the amount of $378,657.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Hill.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CW-013-22":"Resolution No. CW-013-22\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nREORGANIZATION - ABOLISH PLANNING MANAGER/CREATE PLANNER\nECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT\nWHEREAS, a Planning Manager position will become vacant due to an unexpected resignation on June 3, 2022,and\nWHEREAS, the Commissioner of Economic Development has analyzed the needs of the department, evaluated the likely recruitment field, and consulted with the Human Resources Director, Personnel Officer, and County Manager, and\nWHEREAS, it has been determined that reclassifying the vacancy will allow for the greater likelihood of recruiting qualified candidates to meet the operational needs, will provide for growth and development opportunities for a mid-level position, and the duties of the position are most accurately reflected in the Planner job specification, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Planning Manager, CSEA grade 14 position (annualized range of $65,993 to $77,095) will be abolished and a Planner, CSEA grade 11 position (annualized range of $51,870 to $60,624), will be created effective June 4, 2022.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Hill.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","CW-014-22":"Resolution No. CW-014-22\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nALLOCATING ADDITIONAL FUNDING FOR THE\nNIAGARA COUNTY FACADE PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has received funds from the Coronavims State and Local Fiscal Recovery Fund (CSLFRF), a part of the American Rescue Plan Act, and\nWHEREAS, the Coronavims State and Local Fiscal Recovery Fund (CSLFRF), American Rescue Plan Act, Small Business Economic Assistance, provides aid for small businesses to respond to the negative economic impacts of the COVID-19 public health crisis, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature recognizes the importance of exterior capital improvements to individual businesses and business districts, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature adopted resolution ED-003-22 on Febmary 15, 2022 to create and fund the Niagara County Fa9ade Program, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature allocated funding for the redevelopment of small business facades along our commercial corridors, for those businesses which suffered negative economic impact as a result of the COVID-19 public health emergency, and\nWHEREAS, the Department of Economic Development, who is administering the facade program on behalf of Niagara County has seen a tremendous amount of interest in the program, and received over 200 applications for funding, totaling over $5 million, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature would like to support all of the businesses that meet the qualifying criteria of the program, and\nWHEREAS, the Department of Economic Development hereby requests that the Niagara County Legislature allocate an additional $2,234,524 in order to fully fund the Niagara County Fa9ade Program, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.815,44089.07 ARPA State/Local Rec Fund $2,234,524\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.815 74500.01 Contractual $2,234,524\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent- Myers.","ED-014-22":"Resolution No. ED-014-22\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nFIXING DATE AND NOTICE FOR THE SECOND PUBLIC HEARING\nFOR ULTIMATE PHYSIQUE, INC. COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT\nBLOCK GRANT APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the County Legislature wishes to hear public comments relating to the recently completed Community Development Block Grant (\"CDBG\") project supporting the grant funding provided to Ultimate Physique, Inc. at 50 Rogers Avenue in the City ofLockport, New York, and\nWHEREAS, the CDBG program is administered by the New York State Office of Community Renewal (OCR), and provides resources to eligible local governments for housing, economic development, public facilities, public Infrastructure, and planning activities, with the principal purpose of benefitting low/moderate income persons, and\nWHEREAS, OCR requires grant recipients hold a public hearing to provide information to the public and to consider citizen comments regarding CDBG funded projects to help make determinations for the future use of CDBG funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County Legislature hereby calls for a Public Hearing whereat all interested parties shall be heard regarding the CDBG program, the County's commimity development and economic development activities, and the Ultimate Physique, Inc. CDBG project at the Legislature Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York on the 21st day of June, 2022, at 5:40 p.m., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature, at least eight days notice of such hearing shall post a notice upon the bulletin boards in the Courthouse at Lockport, and the Civic Building at Niagara Falls, and shall publish such notice once in the Lockport Union Sun & Journal, and the Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, O Noes, I Absent ~- Myers.","IF-075-22":"Resolution No. IF-075-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION APPROVING RULES AND REGULATIONS\nFOR LOCAL LAW NO. 4 OF 2021\nWHEREAS, Local Law No. 4 of 2021 established Solar Panel Recycling Regulations, and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to Section 9 of said Local Law, the County may adopt rules as necessary for the purpose of implementing, administering and enforcing this Local Law, and\nWHEREAS, Section 5 of the Local law provides for administrative fees from the manufacturer or stewardship organization both for the cost of reviewing the initial stewardship plan, as well as an annual fee for covering the costs of administering, implementing and enforcing the Local Law, and\nWHEREAS, the initial stewardship plans are soon due to the County by the later of August 1, 2022 or within thirty days of its first sale ofaphotovoltaic module in or into the County, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to implement rules for the implementation of this Local Law for these initial plans,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Pursuant to Section 5 of the Local Law, the Administrative Fee to submit the Initial Stewardship Plan is $1000 with an annual fee of $250, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that should a manufacturer be part of a stewardship organization, they must fill out and submit the Stewardship Organization Designation Form, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the submitted plan shall be submitted as one original hard copy and one electronic PDF copy in English, and prior to submission each stewardship organization/manufacturer must have a meeting with the County, which may take place either in person or virtually.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IF-076-22":"Resolution No. IF-076-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nAPPROVAL OF AGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND THE AN-JO BASEBALL LEAGUE\nWHEREAS, the AN-JO Baseball League has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use the existing fields on Davison Road in the City of Lockport for the purpose of a youth baseball league, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the youth and other residents of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the AN-JO Baseball League, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the AN-JO Baseball League as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement with the AN-JO Baseball League.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IF-077-22":"Resolution No. IF-077-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committees.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND THE SQVALLA DISC GOLF CLUB\nWHEREAS, the Squalla Disc Golf Club has requested that the County of Niagara grant them exclusive rights to operate a disc golf tournament in an area situated m the county owned property at Clyde L. Burmaster Park, and Royalton Ravine park, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the residents of Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of the Disc Golf Club to hold a two (2) day event, August 27 and 28, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Squalla Disc Golf Club for this event, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Squalla Disc Golf Club, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IF-078-22":"Resolution No. IF-078-22\nFrom: Infrasfructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nAWARD CONSULTANT CONTRACT\nHESS ROAD BRIDGE OVER TRIBUTARY TO KEG CREEK\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualifled consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Hess Road Bridge over a Tributary to Keg Creek Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H742.15.5197.000 72600.02, Bridges, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Hess Road Bridge over a Tributary to Keg Creek Project be awarded to CHA Consulting Inc., 2200 Main Place Tower, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount not to exceed $230,000 for design, construction administration, and construction inspection, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~- Myers.","IF-079-22":"Resolution No. IF-079-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nAWARD CONSULTANT CONTRACT\nEAST CANAL ROAD BmDGE OVER AN UNNAMED STREAM\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the East Canal Road Bridge over an Unnamed Stream Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H741.15.5197.000 72600.02, Bridges, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the East Canal Road Bridge over an Umiamed Stream Project be awarded to CHA Consulting Inc., 2200 Main Place Tower, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount not to exceed $154,000 for design services, and be it farther\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-025-22":"Resolution No. IL-025-22\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE NORTH TONAWANDA BOTANICAL GARDEN\nORGANIZATION THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the North Tonawanda Botanical Garden Organization, Inc. is a 501(c)3 nonprofit organization established in 2018, and\nWHEREAS, the North Tonawanda Botanical Garden Organization teaches citizens about native plants and pollinators, water wise practices, sustainable gardening and environmental stewardship, and\nWHEREAS, the North Tonawanda Botanical Garden Organization is committed to maintaining the North Tonawanda Botanical Gardens as a scenic destination for the community, now therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the North Tonawanda Botanical Gardens Organization by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: North Tonawanda Botanical Garden Organization $2,700.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~\" Myers.","IL-026-22":"Resolution No. IL-026-22\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17,2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF OLCOTT BEACH CAROUSEL PARK ASSOCIATION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Olcott Beach Carousel Park Association (OBCPA) is a lakeside vintage amusement park that offers families an affordable day of family fun by riding and enjoying restored nostalgic amusement park rides, and\nWHEREAS, the OBCPA annually hosts \"The Park After Dark\" fundraising event on August 9, 2022, which helps raise funds to cover operating costs and improvements to the park, and\nWHEREAS, 2022 proceeds from \"The Park After Dark\" event will be used to refurbish Skyfighter Planes, Rocket Swing Rockets and Kiddie Cars, and\nWHEREAS, the OBCPA requires constant fundraising support in order to provide low cost family entertainment to Niagara County residents and tourists from all over the world, which also benefits local businesses, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports \"The Park After Dark\" fundraising event hosted by Olcott Beach Carousel Park Association by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Olcott Beach Carousel Park Association $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~ Myers.","IL-027-22":"Resolution No. IL-027-22\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF ANCHORING HOPE OF NY, INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Anchoring Hope of NY is a 501 not-for-proflt dedicated to providing opportunities for those affected by mental illness and/or addiction through the use of unique and unconventional sendces, and\nWHEREAS, on September 24, 2022 Anchoring Hope is hosting a walk & basket raffle at Krull Park in order to raise funds to support their mission and expand services across rural Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, funds from the event will expand access to services such as; Quality of Life Scholarships, tamperproof medication boxes, emotional support/service animals, treatment not covered by insurance, and many others, and\nWHEREAS, the COVID pandemic has caused increased stress and anxiety to our residents, especially those struggling with mental health/addiction issues so it is critically important to support our local organizations who are providing semces in our communities to combat these issues, and\nWHEREAS, supporting this event will allow Anchoring Hope ofNY to continue and expand their mission to improve the quality of life for Niagara County residents, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County s Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Anchoring Hope of NY Inc by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Anchoring Hope ofNY, Inc. $500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-029-22":"Resolution No. IL-029-22\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON ART FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Council on the Arts is dedicated to improving the quality of life for all by nurturing a thriving environment for the arts and for cultural activities, promoting opportunities for artists, encouraging public participation in the arts, and actively advocating for the arts, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Art Festival, which is sponsored by the Lewiston Council on the Arts, has become a highlight of the summer season in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Council on the Arts has scheduled the 2022 Lewiston Art Festival for August 13 and 14,2022,and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Art Festival will feature over 175 artists and vendors from across the country and draw thousands of visitors from all over Western New York, Southern Ontario and beyond, and\nWHEREAS, an event of this magnitude can only be successful through full commimity participation and sponsorships, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Art Festival is an economic driver that supports jobs, generates government revenue and is a cornerstone of local tourism, and the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this influx of revenue, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the events and efforts of the Lewiston Council on the Arts by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Lewiston Council on the Arts $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-030-22":"Resolution No. IL-030-22\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON HARVEST & HOPS FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the mission of the Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce is to unify the Niagara River Region and to promote it as a premiere place to live, work and play while promoting tourism, business development, quality of life and the history and culture of the region, and\nWHEREAS, the 2022 Lewiston Hardest & Hops Festival has been planned for September 24 and 25,2022,and\nWHEREAS, this fall street festival will highlight over 135 craft, food/beverage, and farmers market vendors, and\nWHEREAS, an event of this magnitude can only be successful through full community participation and sponsorships, and\nWHEREAS, the festival will draw thousands of visitors to the river region from surrounding Western New York communities and Canada, to the benefit of local businesses, and the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this influx of revenue, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-031-22":"Resolution No. IL-031-22\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE WILSON COMMUNITY LIBRARY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Wilson Community Library is committed to excellent service and providing access to quality materials as a way to empower the community and connect people and ideas to inspire lifelong learning through curiosity and creativity, and\nWHEREAS, the library is currently planning for a Summer Reading Program and would like to be able to run several fun and exciting programs to get children engaged and active at the library while they are out of school for a few months, and\nWHEREAS, the Summer Reading Program will also feature programs for adults as well, and\nWHEREAS, the library would also like to be able to upgrade a few computers as one of the remaining areas in need of improvement, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Wilson Community Library by appropriating monies from said Coimnunity Partnership Fund as follows: Wilson Community Library $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-032-22":"Resolution No. IL-032-22\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE WILSON HISTORICAL SOCIETY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS) the Wilson Historical Society is planning its Memorial Day fair to be held on May 30, 2022, which is their main effort for fimdraising to maintain and upkeep the vast historical collections and campus located in Wilson, and\nWHEREAS, along with many other non-profit organizations, the Historical Society has suffered financially from the devastating effects of the COVID epidemic having been forced to cancel their fairs in 2020 and 2021, and\nWHEREAS, the fair brings thousands of people into the WUson community on Memorial Day weekend all centered around the fair, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County s Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Wilson Historical Society by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Wilson Historical Society $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-033-22":"Resolution No. IL-033-22\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17,2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE WILSON YOUTH BASEBALL LEAGUE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Wilson Youth Baseball is a volunteer-based group that provides softball & baseball instruction for local youth between the ages of 4 and 17; the league usually has an average of 150 to 250 people at their games and events at Krueger Park in Wilson, and\nWHEREAS, the league competes against other youth programs from neighboring communities, and the older teams travel to other local baseball fields as well; the league works hard to provide a fun and educational experience each summer for our local youth and their families, and\nWHEREAS, all donations are put towards improving the condition of their soffcball and baseball fields, improving and adding dugouts on various fields, and purchasing much needed equipment, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County s Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Wilson Youth Baseball League by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Wilson Youth Baseball League $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-035-22":"Resolution No. IL-035-22\nFrom: \u2022 Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17,2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LIONS CLUB OF THE TOWN OF WHEATFIELD, INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club of the Town ofWheatfleld, Inc. is a 501(c)4 non-profit charitable organization who own and maintain the 14 Acre Five Senses Park at 6535 Ward Road which includes a club house, pavilion, gazebo, pergola, and a pond, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club is dedicated to serve those in need in the area, and all funds are dedicated towards delivering the mission s services of the Five Focus Areas; the blind, diabetes, hunger, environment, and pediatric illness (such as cancer), and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club plans to improve their existing pond from over growth and add design features to increase safe access for use, which includes the blind we bring to the site for events. The design includes safety fencing and rails for safety for the blind and the protection of an environmental resource, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club also plans to add a dock/platform to help the blind have access, fish and enjoy their experience safely by the water; the Lions Club also plan to add a fountain to the pond which will provide for aeration, visual aesthetics and acoustic aesthetics for the blind, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club will add a functional safety path to allow/assist the blind to experience a nature walk. The path would leave from the Pavilion, through the pergola, to the pond dock platform, then over the bridge, and into the fields of the 14 acres Five Senses Park; the path would be made of wood chips or playground rubber for safety and reduced maintenance, have railings to guide the blind safely, and have recording units to describe experiences along the way and braille messages, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Lions Club of the Town of Wheatfleld, Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Lions Club of the Town ofWheatfield, Inc. $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-036-22":"Resolution No. IL-036-22\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17,2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF CAMBRIA BOY SCOUT TROOP 8\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Cambria Boy Scout Troop 8 has been in existence for 57 years in Niagara County, and for the last 52 chartered by the Cambria Volunteer Fire Company; currently the troop has 30 boy scouts and 15 active adult leaders and junior leaders, making us one of the largest scout troops in the county, and\nWHEREAS, the troop has graduated nearly 60 Eagle Scouts in its history and each of them has given back in some way to Niagara County through their Eagle Scout Project; these projects have had a significant impact in improving our towns, villages, as well as our town and county parks and many non-profit agencies have benefited from these, and\nWHEREAS, in recent years the troop has become a feeder program for the Cambria Volunteer Fire Company Jr. Firefighting program, and have seen a number of scouts go on to serve in the fire service, and\nWHEREAS, the troop is in need of a complete mechanical overhaul or replacement of a 20 year old tow behind trailer used multiple times a year to transport equipment to campsites both locally and up to 8 hours away from Cambria, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County's Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Cambria Boy Scout Troop 8 by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: Cambria Boy Scout Troop 8 $4,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-037-22":"Resolution No. IL-037-22\nFrom: Legislator Christopher Voccio and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF LIGHTHOUSE WOMEN AND CHILDREN OF NIAGARA FALLS\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Lighthouse Women and Children of Niagara Falls is an important organization that serves many residents in many ways, and\nWHEREAS, Lighthouse Women and Children of Niagara Falls operates multiple programs that serve women and children in need, and\nWHEREAS, Lighthouse Women and Children provides these much needed services at a very low cost per person, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County s Community Partnership Fund was established in the 2022 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the good works of Lighthouse Women and Children of Niagara Falls 'and its programming by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: - Lighthouse Women and Children , $500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-038-22":"Resolution No. IL-038-22\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Anthony J. Nemi, Randy R. Bradt, Jeffrey Elder, Shawn A. Elder and Parks, Recreation & Tourism Ad Hoc Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nSUPPORT FOR LOCKPORrS RAILYARD SKATE PARK 2022 PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Power Coalition (\"NPC\") and the New York Power Authority (NYPA) agreed to a licensing settlement for the next 50 years for the Niagara Power Project, and\nWHEREAS, that settlement allows Niagara County to receive, through NPC Host Community Standing Committee (\"HCSC\"), up to $390,000 annually to fund projects which are found to be consistent with the Niagara Greenway projects along the Greenway Trail, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport Community Services, Inc. has made application to the Niagara County's Ad Hoc Committee on Parks, Recreation and Tourism for Greenway funds for the Lockport's Railyard Skate Park 2022 Project which consists of the construction of a concrete skateboarding park, and\nWHEREAS, the Railyard Skateboarding Park, is a wooden above ground park that opened in the spring of 2007 near the community pool at Outwater Memorial Park in Lockport to meet the recreational needs of the youth in the community, and\nWHEREAS, since its opening, the Railyard Skate Park has been utilized by many as the interest in skateboarding and BMX has increased dramatically over the years, and\nWHEREAS, the costs of repairing the wooden structure have been problematic and at times expensive for skate surface materials, and\nWHEREAS, in the spring of 2019, the Friends of the Railyard Skate Park and Lockport Community Services, Inc., along with support of the City ofLockport, received a matching grant from the Tony Hawk (now known as The Skate Park Project) and Ralph C. Wilson, Jr. Foundation's Built to Play initiative, which provided matching grants for 20 communities in Western New York and eastern Michigan to build concrete public skate parts, and\nWHEREAS, in November, 2019, Lockport was chosen as one of two projects in the latest cycle of funding support, and the $300,000 matching grant requires awardees to raise up to $250,000 in matching funding with $50,000 not required to be matched, but to be directed at environmental impact efforts; to date $182,000 of that match of the goal has been raised, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport's Railyard Skate Park Project 2022 will feature an 11,000 square-foot concrete skate park and will be a destination site for local residents as well as visitors and families from across the region, which will tie the Railyard Park to the Erie Canal Trailway and Greenway bike paths to residents, visitors and stakeholder groups, and will also enhance the Outwater Park complex by complimenting the community pool, the new Frisbee golf course, tennis and basketball courts, baseball diamond and the football field, and\nWHEREAS, the Lockporfs Railyard Skate Park 2022 Project meets the guiding principles of the Niagara River Greenway, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport Community Services, Inc. has requested $30,000 in its application which is to be utilized for the construction ofLockport's Railyard Skate Park 2022 Project, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport's Railyard Skate Park 2022 Project has the support and approval of Niagara County Legislator Anthony J. Nemi, City of Lockport Mayor Michelle Roman, the Grigg Lewis Foundation, Inc., and Friends of the Railyard, and\nWHEREAS, the Ad Hoc Committee for Parks, Recreation and Tourism Fund appointed by the Niagara County Legislature to review projects brought to Niagara County seeking funding from Niagara County through the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee, recommends that Niagara County approve and support this project, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport's Railyard Skate Park 2022 Project has utilized great effort and is complimented for being able to receive other funding as well as in kind services, and\nWHEREAS, after receipt ofin-kind services and other funding, the remaining $30,000 needed for the project is being requested, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby support and sponsor the funding of $30,000.00, subject to receipt ofNYPA funding for Greenway projects in July, 2022, for Lockport's Railyard Skate Park 2022 Project when presented to the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-039-22":"Resolution No. IL-039-22\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Anthony J. Nemi, Randy R. Bradt, Jeffrey Elder, Shawn A. Elder and Parks, Recreation & Tourism Ad Hoc Committee.\nDated: May 17, 2022\nSUPPORT FOR NIAGARA COUNTY COMMUNITY COLLEGE\nVETERANS MEMOmAL PARK PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Power Coalition (\"NPC\") and the New York Power Authority (NYPA) agreed to a licensing settlement for the next 50 years for the Niagara Power Project, and\nWHEREAS, that settlement allows Niagara County to receive, through NPC Host Community Standing Committee (\"HCSC\"), up to $390,000 annually to fund projects which are found to be consistent with the Niagara Greenway projects along the Greenway Trail, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Community College (NCCC) has made application to the Niagara County's Ad Hoc Committee on Parks, Recreation and Tourism for Greenway funds for the Niagara County Community College Veterans Memorial Park Project, and\nWHEREAS, the NCCC Veterans Memorial Park Project consists of the relocation and reconstruction of the Veterans Memorial Park, which is currently located at Niagara County Community College's Sanbom campus, and\nWHEREAS, the existing NCCC Veterans Memorial which honors military veterans throughout Niagara County, was originally developed and constructed between 1973 and 1979 and although it is an important piece of history atNCCC, it is in need of significant upgrades to its design, and\nWHEREAS, the NCCC Veterans Memorial Park Project will be fully reconstructed, relocated and expanded to increase its visibility and accessibly on campus by moving it to a site adjacent to a nearby main campus parking lot and will feature a safe, short accessible walkway leading from the main Campus Lot 3 to the memorial with ample parking, a large area for congregation with four benches, a Remembrance Wall commemorating service heroes and a Wall of Honor with the logos and emblems of all branches of the military, and WHERAS, the new location will offer a prominent extension between the bike path around the perimeter of the campus to nearby State Bike Route 5, which will offer ample access for the community, and solar lighting will prominently display the site just off the main campus parking lot, and\nWHEREAS, the NCCC Veterans Memorial Park Project meets the guiding principles of the Niagara River Greenway and will raise the consciousness of visitors by providing the community with a centralized location to celebrate those who have served and also serve as a destination for ceremonies and military holidays while offering distinction to the military members of Western New York, and\nWHEREAS, the NCCC Veterans Memorial Park Project has requested $100,000 in its application which is to be utilized for the construction of the NCCC Veterans Memorial Park Project, and\nWHEREAS, the NCCC Veterans Memorial Park Project has the support and approval of New York State Senator Robert G. Ortt, New York State Assemblyman Angelo J. Morinello, Niagara County Manager Richard E. Updegrove, Niagara County Legislator David E. Godfrey, Niagara County Legislator Jesse P. Gooch, City of Niagara Falls Mayor Robert M. Restaino, Niagara Wheatfleld Central School District Superintendent Daniel Ljiljanich, Tonawanda Sportsmen's Club, Veterans One-Stop Center of Western New York, Western New York Heroes, Inc., Tortuga Sandwich Shop, Tops Friendly Markets of Sanborn, Subway, as well as various local Veterans, and\nWHEREAS, the Ad Hoc Committee for Parks, Recreation and Tourism Fund appointed by the Niagara County Legislature to review projects brought to Niagara County seeking funding from Niagara County through the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee, recommends that Niagara County approve and support this project, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Community College Veterans Memorial Park Project has utilized great effort and is complimented for being able to receive other funding as well as in kind services, and\nWHEREAS, after receipt ofin-kind services and other funding, the remaining $100,000 needed for the project is being requested, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby support and sponsor the funding of $100,000.00, subject to receipt ofNYPA funding for Greenway projects in July, 2022, for the Niagara County Community College Veterans Memorial Park Project when presented to the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-040-22":"Resolution No. IL-040-22\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Anthony J. Nemi, Randy R. Bradt, Jeffrey Elder, Shawn A. Elder and Parks, Recreation & Tourism Ad Hoc Committee.\nDated: May 17,2022\nSUPPORT FOR THE WHEATFIELD LIONS NATURE TRAIL\n& FISHING POND PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Power Coalition (\"NPC\") and the New York Power Authority agreed to a licensing settlement for the next 50 years for the Niagara Power Project, and\nWHEREAS, that settlement allows Niagara County to receive, through NPC Host Community Standing Committee (\"HCSC\"), up to $390,000 annually to fund projects which are found to be consistent with the Niagara Greenway projects along the Greenway Trail, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club of the Town of Wheatfield has made application to Niagara County's Ad Hoc Committee on Parks, Recreation and Tourism for Greenway funds to support its Wheatfield Lions Nature Trail & Fishing Pond Project, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club of the Town of Wheatfield project would expand the efforts to improve and develop the community's Five Senses Park in order to benefit the blind, handicapped and disabled, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club of the Town of Wheatfield will now undertake the Wheatfleld Lions Nature Trail & Fishing Pond Project, which dramatically redesigns the Wheatfield Lion's Five Senses Park located at 6535 Ward Road in Wheatfleld, with the purpose of improving the existing pond from over growth, design safety fencing and rails to allow the blind to have access, add a functional safety path to allow/assist the blind to experience a nature walk, and to integrate a play area along the trail, and\nWHEREAS, the Lions Club of the Town ofWheatfield is requesting $1 15,000, of the total project cost, and\nWHEREAS, Wheatfield Lions Nature Trail & Fishing Pond Project meets the guiding principles of the Niagara River Greenway and will enhance the trails of the Niagara River Greenway and connect to other Greenway Projects along State Bike Route 5, and\nWHEREAS, the Ad Hoc Committee for Parks, Recreation and Tourism appointed by the Niagara County Legislature to review projects brought to Niagara County seeking funding from Niagara County through the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee, has recommended that Niagara County approve and support this project, and\nWHEREAS, the Wheatfleld Lions Nature Trail & Fishing Pond Project has the support and approval of the Niagara County Manager Richard E. Updegrove, and Niagara County Legislator Jesse P. Gooch, and\nWHEREAS, the Town ofWheatfield has sent the Lions Club ofWheatfield a letter of support in their efforts to improve, change and integrate safety of the Five Senses Park for the blind, disabled and handicap, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby support and sponsor the funding of $115,000.00, subject to receipt ofNYPA Funding for Greenway Projects in July, 2022, for the Wheatfield Lions Nahire Trail & Fishing Pond Project when presented to the Niagara Power Coalition and the Host Community Standing Committee.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers.","IL-041-22":"Resolution No. IL-041-22\nFrom: Legislators Shawn A. Foti, Richard E. Abbott, Michael A. Hill, Irene M. Myers, David E. Godfrey, William J. Collins, Anthony J. Neml, Richard L. Andres, Jesse P. Gooch, Randy R. Bradt, Christopher Voccio and Rebecca J. Wydysh..\nDated: May 17, 2022\nA RESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE NIAGARA COUNTY FAIRGROUNDS ACCESSIBILITY\nPROJECT\nWHEREAS, since 1913, Comell Cooperative Extension of Niagara County (CCE-Niagara) has responded to the needs of local residents with unbiased, research-based information, tools and education that people have come to depend on and trust, and has run programs that are developed in direct response to community input, based on the most current information available from Comell and other Land Grant universities from across the nation, and\nWHEREAS, CCE-Niagara also hosts the annual Niagara County Fair, which draws approximately 35,000 people to the fairgrounds in Lockport for the five-day event filled with local events, live music, shows, contests, food, rides and much more, and\nWHEREAS, due to their importance to our community, including serving as an early voting location each election season, CCE-Niagara has been recognized as a partner agency by the Niagara County Legislature for the purposes of county financial support, and\nWHEREAS, CCE-Niagara is now undertaking a Niagara County Fairgrounds Accessibility Project, as the walkways and driveways are in significant need of repair, and\nWHEREAS, CCE-Niagara seeks county support for a portion of this project that are essential to running large events, like the fair, and are in response to fair patrons who have repeatedly expressed concern about improving access to the fairgrounds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature, using American Rescue Plan funds, wishes to support the Niagara County Fairgrounds Accessibility Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.10.1989.110 44089.07 ARPA State/Locai Rec Fund $125,000\nINCREASE EXPENSE:\nA.10.1989.110 74525.03 Comell Cooperative Ext $125,000\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Abbott, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Abbott.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Myers."};
